00:00:29.229 --> 00:00:31.815
We recently did an episode
about nematodes,

00:00:32.182 --> 00:00:37.220
the phylum of worm that outnumbers
just about every animal on this planet.

00:00:37.670 --> 00:00:39.706
Now, it's not the most striking of animals,

00:00:39.706 --> 00:00:43.309
but the nematode has had a few
distinguished scientific decades,

00:00:43.610 --> 00:00:46.312
thanks to its many uses in laboratories.

00:00:46.813 --> 00:00:47.897
So as far as worms go,

00:00:47.897 --> 00:00:51.818
the nematode seems to dominate
much of our scientific understanding.

00:00:51.818 --> 00:00:57.790
But worms, despite their seemingly simple
bodies, are a diverse bunch.

00:00:58.007 --> 00:01:03.229
Which is why we thought for today it might 
be fun to visit with a less famous worm

00:01:03.229 --> 00:01:06.282
and like one of those relatives
that you don't really know very much about

00:01:06.432 --> 00:01:10.186
But every time you see them,
there's a new strange story to unpack

00:01:14.574 --> 00:01:19.178
They are the Aeolosomatids, 
a family of freshwater worms.

00:01:19.545 --> 00:01:22.148
The ones that you see here are invaders.

00:01:22.215 --> 00:01:25.268
They showed up uninvited
in a blepharisma culture that

00:01:25.268 --> 00:01:29.055
James, our master of microscopes,
has been taken care of for a long time.

00:01:29.439 --> 00:01:33.226
And while Aeolosoma worms are
you know, worms,

00:01:33.226 --> 00:01:36.829
they are in a different class of worm
because as we have seen before,

00:01:36.829 --> 00:01:40.066
there are, in fact, many ways to be a tube.

00:01:40.066 --> 00:01:43.603
Where nematodes are roundworms,
Aeolosoma are segmented,

00:01:43.603 --> 00:01:47.857
placing them in the Annelid phylum,
along with earthworms and leeches,

00:01:48.374 --> 00:01:51.711
 Aeolosoma are usually several millimeters in length,

00:01:51.711 --> 00:01:54.981
their bodies divided into more
than ten segments that you can see

00:01:54.981 --> 00:01:59.268
scrunching up and expanding as the worm
wiggles its way through the microcosmos.

00:02:01.454 --> 00:02:04.307
And the Aeolosoma are striking to look at.

00:02:04.457 --> 00:02:07.760
You can see their organs
through their transparent bodies,

00:02:07.994 --> 00:02:12.915
and as it moves, bundles of long bristly
hairs wave along the side of its body.

00:02:13.433 --> 00:02:17.236
Those hairs mark the Aeolosoma
as a specific type of an annelid

00:02:17.420 --> 00:02:19.939
called a polychaete, or bristle worm.

00:02:20.690 --> 00:02:24.577
Some bristle worms are found
in unusual places like hydrothermal vents,

00:02:24.977 --> 00:02:28.581
but our Aeolosoma come
from a much more mundane home.

00:02:28.965 --> 00:02:32.201
They're usually found in bodies
of fresh water where they'd like to crawl

00:02:32.201 --> 00:02:35.354
among the leaves and algae
that settle at the bottom of the water.

00:02:35.888 --> 00:02:39.292
And inside their bodies
are colorful gland cells,

00:02:39.408 --> 00:02:42.528
though no one is really sure
what those cells exist for

00:02:42.528 --> 00:02:45.164
or why they have their particular colors.

00:02:45.581 --> 00:02:48.701
And some species, the cells are green
and others they're yellow.

00:02:48.701 --> 00:02:51.704
And sometimes, 
as with our worms, they're red.

00:02:52.088 --> 00:02:54.891
The final result is a worm
that looks a little like it

00:02:54.891 --> 00:02:58.411
ran into a porcupine
while also having caught chicken pox.

00:03:01.981 --> 00:03:05.718
While there are some Aeolosoma
species that reproduce sexually,

00:03:05.718 --> 00:03:09.922
most reproduce asexually dividing
to form a copy of itself.

00:03:10.239 --> 00:03:12.992
The Aeolosoma creates its clone at its end,

00:03:13.309 --> 00:03:16.779
linking the old and new versions of itself
like a chain.

00:03:17.079 --> 00:03:19.949
You can see the new Aeolosoma here,

00:03:19.949 --> 00:03:23.502
looking like it's attached to the other’s butt because,

00:03:23.502 --> 00:03:25.187
it's attached to the other’s butt.

00:03:25.721 --> 00:03:29.041
And this chain can keep going
as the Aeolosoma

00:03:29.041 --> 00:03:32.862
keeps dividing, adding more worms
that are connected together

00:03:32.862 --> 00:03:36.048
so that the final length
of their combined bodies sometimes

00:03:36.048 --> 00:03:38.918
reaches around ten millimeters total.

00:03:38.918 --> 00:03:41.170
That's ten millimeters of clones

00:03:41.170 --> 00:03:46.192
combined to create one giant mega worm
until eventually the chain breaks

00:03:46.609 --> 00:03:48.611
and they all go their separate ways.

00:03:50.629 --> 00:03:52.148
So when James found these worms

00:03:52.148 --> 00:03:55.367
invading his samples, you'd think
maybe this would be an exciting find.

00:03:55.634 --> 00:03:59.972
Here is a culture full of bristled,
polka-dotted, chain-forming clones.

00:03:59.989 --> 00:04:01.457
What could be more exciting!

00:04:02.575 --> 00:04:06.729
Well, as wonderful as they are to 
look at these invasions are not ideal

00:04:06.762 --> 00:04:10.216
because they are also essentially vacuum cleaners.

00:04:10.483 --> 00:04:14.086
Their mouths are lined with cilia
that wave around and help the worms

00:04:14.086 --> 00:04:16.989
suction up bits of plant and animal debris.

00:04:16.989 --> 00:04:17.857
When they're in a pond,

00:04:17.873 --> 00:04:21.343
They like to crawl across leaves
and algae for their meals.

00:04:21.577 --> 00:04:22.311
But when you find them

00:04:22.311 --> 00:04:25.481
in bottles of ciliate cultures,
you've been lovingly maintaining,

00:04:25.748 --> 00:04:27.850
that's when things get a bit dicier. 

00:04:27.850 --> 00:04:29.685
Because Aeolosoma will eat

00:04:29.685 --> 00:04:33.339
just about anything, including each other.

00:04:34.540 --> 00:04:35.207
Indeed.

00:04:35.207 --> 00:04:36.675
in one very dramatic scene

00:04:36.675 --> 00:04:41.630
documented in 1901 scientists
observing the species Aeolosoma tenebrarum

00:04:41.630 --> 00:04:43.999
described the way
these chains of worms

00:04:43.999 --> 00:04:48.370
would twist up in each other,
creating a writhing, tangled ball of worms

00:04:48.370 --> 00:04:51.323
that would stay stuck together
for long periods of time.

00:04:51.740 --> 00:04:55.010
And when the scientists pulled
these balls apart, they usually found

00:04:55.010 --> 00:04:57.646
at least one worm
that had been partially eaten.

00:04:58.280 --> 00:05:01.150
I'm sure the etiquette around
cannibalistic frenzies varies,

00:05:01.150 --> 00:05:04.520
but for most animals, getting eaten
by another member of your species

00:05:04.520 --> 00:05:06.922
would seem, at the very least,
a little rude.

00:05:07.306 --> 00:05:10.342
But when you're Aeolosoma, 
it's not that big of a deal.

00:05:10.609 --> 00:05:13.162
Honestly, it's not much 
more than an inconvenience,

00:05:13.162 --> 00:05:16.732
because if a part of it gets eaten,
it can always regenerate

00:05:18.033 --> 00:05:23.889
In one case, the scientists watching these 
balls of worms found that one worm had its head eaten.

00:05:23.906 --> 00:05:27.643
But in about three days
it was able to make a new one.

00:05:28.210 --> 00:05:32.414
It would probably have taken less time
to regenerate other parts of their body-

00:05:32.414 --> 00:05:35.117
heads seemed to take the Aeolosoma a bit longer,

00:05:35.317 --> 00:05:38.487
perhaps because of all the complex parts
that need to be rebuilt.

00:05:38.487 --> 00:05:42.825
And the Aeolosoma can regenerate even 
when it is cut into multiple segments.

00:05:43.492 --> 00:05:47.763
This superpower has made one species
called Aeolosoma viride

00:05:47.763 --> 00:05:49.965
particularly interesting to scientists.

00:05:50.416 --> 00:05:52.418
And it's not just
that they can regenerate.

00:05:52.418 --> 00:05:56.538
After all, as incredible as this ability
is, there are plenty of other animals

00:05:56.538 --> 00:05:58.340
that can regenerate as well.

00:05:58.340 --> 00:06:02.478
But scientists aren't just interested
in how animals regenerate.

00:06:02.711 --> 00:06:05.597
They also want to know how
those regenerative abilities

00:06:05.781 --> 00:06:08.117
change as the animal gets older.

00:06:08.767 --> 00:06:11.770
That's a difficult question to study
because as you might expect,

00:06:11.987 --> 00:06:15.641
self-healing animals have often, pretty long lifespans.

00:06:15.974 --> 00:06:20.562
So it's a challenge to wait years
or even decades to study how their ability

00:06:20.562 --> 00:06:24.616
to regenerate changes
with the wear and tear of aging.

00:06:25.417 --> 00:06:28.604
Aeolosoma viride
however, has a lifespan

00:06:28.720 --> 00:06:32.741
of only about two months,
which means it goes from young to old

00:06:32.741 --> 00:06:36.912
on a manageable timescale for scientists
cycling through experiments.

00:06:37.095 --> 00:06:40.449
And that makes it a useful organism
to observe how that capacity

00:06:40.449 --> 00:06:43.752
to rebuild itself changes
as the worm ages.

00:06:44.536 --> 00:06:47.172
But as useful as regeneration
is for survival,

00:06:47.389 --> 00:06:50.058
it is not the only tool
the worm relies on.

00:06:50.359 --> 00:06:54.913
After all, not all dangers can easily
be patched up by rebuilding body parts.

00:06:55.130 --> 00:06:59.651
Sometimes the worm has to preempt
dangerous conditions, and for that

00:06:59.901 --> 00:07:01.603
it turns to the cyst.

00:07:05.774 --> 00:07:10.178
In nature, the worms will likely begin
forming these cysts in autumn,

00:07:10.178 --> 00:07:14.633
when the water gets cold and begins to fill
with the remains of decomposing life.

00:07:15.100 --> 00:07:18.937
And as the temperatures continue
to fall, the worms begin to slow down,

00:07:19.187 --> 00:07:24.226
crawling to areas full of delicious debris
for them to stock up on, and eventually,

00:07:24.409 --> 00:07:27.679
the worms begin to secrete a mucus,
creating a gooey

00:07:27.679 --> 00:07:30.649
shell that then hardens into a cyst.

00:07:31.099 --> 00:07:36.405
You can see the granules of red pigment
swirling around as the worm moves inside.

00:07:36.738 --> 00:07:37.839
Some of that activity

00:07:37.839 --> 00:07:42.194
might be the peristaltic movement
of its intestines, but it's also possible

00:07:42.194 --> 00:07:46.932
that the warmth of the microscope lamp
is causing the worms to stir as well.

00:07:47.482 --> 00:07:48.733
And in their ponds,

00:07:48.733 --> 00:07:53.054
when warm weather comes, the worm
will get ready to emerge from its encased

00:07:53.088 --> 00:07:56.992
hibernation, using its head
to push at the hardened case of its cyst

00:07:57.342 --> 00:08:00.378
until it manages to poke a hole through
from which it can escape.

00:08:00.829 --> 00:08:01.997
It can take a worm anywhere

00:08:01.997 --> 00:08:04.966
from 30 minutes
to several hours to make its exit.

00:08:04.966 --> 00:08:08.086
And if there's a thick
coating of bacteria on the cyst,

00:08:08.086 --> 00:08:10.972
it may even take the worms several days.

00:08:11.439 --> 00:08:14.709
And from there well,
it is a life of suction,

00:08:14.709 --> 00:08:17.779
feeding and chain
link clones and regenerating.

00:08:18.013 --> 00:08:21.917
Perhaps not normal to us,
but what's normal anyway?

00:08:22.300 --> 00:08:24.586
Especially when you're a worm.

00:08:26.888 --> 00:08:29.841
Thank you for coming on this journey
with us as we explore 

00:08:29.841 --> 00:08:32.060
the unseen world that surrounds us.

